The goroutines capture the loop variable `i` by reference. In Go 1.21, the `for` loop reuses the same `i` variable for every iteration, so by the time the goroutines run, the loop has finished and `i` is `5`. That’s why every goroutine prints `5`.

Capture the value once per iteration, for example by passing it as a parameter to the goroutine.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"sync"
)

func main() {
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(i int) {
			defer wg.Done()
			fmt.Println(i)
		}(i)
	}
	wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

