The main problems are:

1. `if data[i] == max_val` compares the current element to the best value seen so far, not to the previous element/current run. Once the data changes to a value that is not the current maximum, `current_count` gets reset repeatedly instead of counting the new run. In `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`, after switching from `2` to `1`, `max_val` is still `2`, so the run of `1`s is never accumulated.

2. A run is only committed when a different following element is found. The final run is never checked after the loop ends, so a longest streak at the end of the input is ignored. In this example, the longest streak is the final run of three `1`s.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_val = data[0];
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == current_val {
            current_count += 1;
        } else {
            if current_count > max_count {
                max_count = current_count;
                max_val = current_val;
            }

            current_val = data[i];
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }

    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = current_val;
    }

    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

